<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">chop</ansi>

Fell a tree and cut it into logs.

  <ansi fg="command">chop</ansi>            fell whatever grows here
  <ansi fg="command">chop <tree></ansi>     the same, checking the trees are what you expect

You need an <ansi fg="yellow">axe</ansi>. A hatchet will do as a crude one, but a woodcutter's axe is
far better and a steel felling axe better still (<ansi fg="command">help tools</ansi>). Felling takes
a few rounds; harder woods take longer, and a better axe is quicker.

Felling is labour: your <ansi fg="yellow">Strength</ansi> and <ansi fg="yellow">Vitality</ansi> and your axe decide how it
goes, not any skill. A strong woodcutter gets more logs from each tree. The
logs are graded like any other material, and your axe caps the grade.
A felled tree may also give branches (whittle them into arrow shafts) and
bark (oak bark and birch bark tan leather; pine gives pitch).

Logs are heavy. You will not carry many at once, so plan the trip home, or
sell them at the Cascade Pass lumber camp.

Every forest room holds only so many good trees. Fell them all and the stand
is cut back to stumps until it regrows, about one tree an hour, and it may
grow back as something else. Woods next door tend to grow the same trees.

